A||||21||Motorhead||The header saga continues!||||||
Z||000000||Motorhead||04-15-99||05:31 PM||||Twenty four hours later, I've found a manufacturer of header flanges for the IRON DUKE. <p>Hey Ogre! The head on this car also SUCKS! The "crossflow head" shares exhaust ports between cylinders 2 and 3,which means a header with any credibility must utilize a three into one collector. No way around it!<p>Holy shades of Kawasaki!<p>I knew this allready, but it just didn't click.<p>That's OK though, with proper tube sizing, this can be overcome.<p>Talked to my lead welder at work, and the fabrication is no problem. The trick is going to be tube sizing. <p>With 1 1/2" tubing on cylinders 1 and 4, and 1 3/4" tubing on cylinders 2 and 3, collecting into a 2 1/2" exhaust system,their should be plenty of room for air to move for low RPM torque, and high revs, if you call 4500RPM high.<p>Help guys! I'm grasping for knowledgeble insight. <p>Still waiting for a response from Hooker.
Z||000001||theogre||04-16-99||08:10 AM||||I hadn't had the manifold off since last time it cracked..... I had forgoten about the port being shared. I wonder if there's a way to effectivly alter it without restricting it. If it's open enough in there a skilled welder maybe could put in a seperator. The metal for the wall, and the welding rod, would need to be real close to cast in expansion or it could crack out, or even crack the head. Welding could even give you a bit of shaping at the corners.<p>if there's enough material in that area of the head, you could mill a slot into the port and just slide in a wall. that would allow for diferant expansions. You could use a piece of stainless for the wall.<p>I don't have access to a metalergist, and I can't see a head for a while, so these are just posible ideas to look at.<br>
Z||000002||Patrick||04-16-99||02:13 PM||||Hi Motorhead,<p>This is just a small point in regards to the tubing size. If cylinders 2 & 3 "share" exhaust ports, why would there be a need to have <b>larger</b> tubes for those particular cylinders (as you have stated)? If I follow you correctly, it would seem that exhaust restriction for cylinders 2 & 3 would already be <b>less</b> because exhaust pressure would be relieved by exiting through the other cylinder's header tube. Does that sound reasonable?<br>    
Z||000003||Motorhead||04-16-99||03:04 PM||||Wow! Now for some brain straining!<br>I think your'e right Patrick.<br>Firing order is 1-3-4-2, so when cylinder 2 valves are open, cylinder 3 valves are closed, and vice versa. So in actuallity, the two cylinders never SHARE exhaust space.<br>So obvious, yet so easily missed by a novice! Tube size makes no difference. Guess I should be talking to the engineering department at work, I thought all they did all day was drink coffee and talk about their Sunday golf scores!<br>Thanks Patrick, back to the drawing board.<p>  
Z||000004||theogre||04-20-99||09:55 PM||||I was thinking about this some more over the last couple days....... I realised that a "wall" idea probably would be a problem..... the EGR port is in there, and a wall might mess with that.<p>1342..... Hmmmmm that might mean only a 3 tube header which is easier to make. I think to reduce turbulence you might need to funnel the center one, at least a little...... Tuning the header would be a bit of a bitch. If I understand tuned headers correctly, you need volume matching as much as correct lenght so that center tube isn't going to be just cut the same size as the other 2. Even w/o tuning it should breath better than that freak thing that GM slapped together. that thing has to be horridly turbulent. ((oddly a turbo would love it on that manifold. turbo's don't like tuned headers much. There's even room for one, and the stock exhaust could be retained with only slight mods. Now if the engine wouldn't fly apart in that application we'd be all set..... ))<p>Too bad we cant afford the HD heads.... it has 4 individual ports as far as I can tell.<p>((A "tuned" header uses the pulses from each tube to create a slight vacuum in the others as the pulse passes out the collector. this vacuum improves cylinder scavaging. For this to work, the pulses must "phase" corrrectly in time. You want a pluse going thru the collector just slightly ahead of the Ex valve opening so that the vacuum pulse created peaks with the valve. It's quite literaly tuned like a musical instrument.)) 
Z||000005||Motorhead||04-21-99||03:10 PM||||Your explanation on scavenging sounds good Ogre, that's how I understand it too. This is probably beyond my capabilities though. I'd settle for just improved flow right now!<p>TURBO- Right now I'm running 7psi boost on my turbo-Z, detonation keeps trying to rear it's ugly head though. Next addition to that car is an intercooler to fix that. However, any increase in manifold pressure on the Fiero scares the bejesus out of me,I've seen engine internals scattered along the road before from my last Iron Duke motor, not a pretty sight! Damage was easy to asses through the hole in the block though!<p>By the way, in my research I saw a price for the Super Duty engine. Too obscene to repeat!<p> <br>
Z||000006||HF6582||04-21-99||10:59 PM||||if you have headers built for your car,<br>make sure they have equal length primaries.<br>you'll end up with more lowend torque <br>(more streetable).
Z||000007||Motorhead||04-22-99||02:53 PM||||Thanks HF. I've put it in my notes.
Z||000008||theogre||04-22-99||06:21 PM||||HF,<p>equal length works for heads that have individulal openings for each port. It's the fast way to get a tuned header. In Fiero's 4 banger head, equal Volume of the middle to the ends is the goal. The shared middle port really makes tuning a pain.<p>The only way I can think of to get equal volume, is to CC the ports. Not a fun project.
Z||000009||HF6582||04-23-99||12:28 AM||||ogre,<br>you are right about that.  i wasn't thinking<br>about the shared middle port,  but they<br>should work fine considering the firing<br>order.  the only problem would be in the <br>higher rpms when exhaust pulses are closer<br>together.
Z||000010||Motorhead||04-25-99||05:59 AM||||After a week of trying to work out a header for this car, I'm sitting here with all the parts to put my engine back together, and no satisfactory solution to replacing the piece of crap I've got to bolt up to the exhaust side of this motor!<p>On the intake side, the manifold and head have been cleaned up to improve flow, and the exhaust system has been shortened after removal of the converter and muffler.(tip resonators are being used for sound control,it's either going to sound GREAT, or like an old John Deere tractor, I'll let ya'll know!)<p>Air supply - I should receive a set of lower rear quarter panels this week which will receive a pair of NACA scoops. Left side will supply air for engine induction, right side will supply air for engine compartment cooling. <p>Keep up the header input, I'm still working on this problem, but the price to build keeps going up! The 3 into 1 collector is available as a custom fabrication from a manufacturer, but adds more money to this project.<p><p>
Z||000011||Patrick||04-25-99||05:02 PM||||Motorhead,<p>From what you've been reporting lately, I get the impression that you've given up any thought on using the Hooker header for the 2.5L 4-banger. Why is this? Is it no longer available? Is it "too" expensive? If it's the expense, are you not beginning to find out that designing and assembling a header from scratch can be also be an expensive procedure?<p>In another thread at this forum, there are people discussing various "solutions" to installing headers on the V-6. Maybe I'm just a little too practical, but I think that there's a lot more to building a set of headers than simply bending and welding a few pipes together. I'm NOT suggesting this is all that you were intending to do, but I do get the impression that there are a few contributors to this forum who think that designing and assembling a set of headers from scratch is nothing more than a "weekend" project. I'd love to be proved wrong, but I believe it's a complicated task that is beyond the capabilities of most backyard mechanics.<p>Continue to keep us updated on your progress. I'm finding your story interesting.<p>
Z||000012||Motorhead||04-26-99||02:22 AM||||Hey Patrick<p>Cost is definitely an issue, as I've located the different components for fabrication, the price has steadily climbed to the point where I've postponed because of the money allready invested in the rest of the car. I need room in the budget for new paint and some goodies for the suspension still. <p>I still can't seem to locate a set of Hookers, but haven't recieved a reply from them yet as to availability or price.<p>I agree with your observation that this is not a weekend project, however the cost is a bit higher than I expected.<p>I haven't given up though.    
Z||000013||Patrick||05-11-99||12:59 AM||||Hi Motorhead,<p>I thought I'd resurrect this thread because you've got your Fiero back on the road, but you have NOT mentioned one thing about the exhaust system. <p>So, what's the scoop?<p>PS. You never commented on my Jim Morrison joke in the other thread. Hey, it was for your benefit. My feelings are hurt now. <IMG SRC="http://home.att.net/~albpm/smiled2.gif"> <p>
Z||000014||Motorhead||05-12-99||02:43 PM||||Sorry Patrick, got sidetracked over not tightening the distributor bolt!<p>The exhaust system is still using the stock manifold, with custom bent pipe straight to a pair of tip resonators, it's cat-less.<p>Sounds pretty good,not excessively loud, but just enough to let you know how she feels when the windows are down.<p>I think the porting and lack of the cat has made for an easier reving engine with slightly improved acceleration. Not to mention the shorter exhaust length.<p>Haven't given up on a header, just occupied with the outside appearance right now. Got a set of extra rear, lower quarter panels for installation of a pair of NACA scoops, Should have the new panels ready to install in a couple of weeks or so. alot going on at work right now.<p>By the way, I was pretty impressed that you knew who Jim Morrison was, I thought all you young guys listened to was Wrap through those overabundant sub-woofers! Bet y'all spend alot of time re-torquing loose bolts!<p><p>  <br>
